\p{Reading: the arena CPUs say the same as the efficiency cores (2.2), at half the absolute level and with less scatter in the one-worker cells:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Parallel and G1 = Serial} within 3 \% in every group (CPU 0.99 / 1.00 with the machine at one worker, 1.01 / 1.00 with the reference engine, 1.00 / 1.00 and 1.03 / 1.01 at eight workers).}
\li{\strong{ZGC: +4 to +7 \% CPU time} (1.04, 1.06, 1.07, 1.06), +3 to +11 \% instructions, and a wall clock at eight workers of 1.20 and 1.21 of Serial's, because it commits the 8 GB heap before the checker starts.}
\li{\strong{Shenandoah: +15 to +21 \% CPU time, generational +15 to +26 \%} (1.15 / 1.15, 1.21 / 1.19, 1.17 / 1.16, 1.19 / 1.26), +20 to +31 \% instructions, and a wall clock at eight workers of 1.23 / 1.22 of Serial's.}
\li{\strong{No collector improves the wall clock with eight workers}: the geometric means of the 8-worker wall ratios are 1.05 / 1.02 (Parallel) and 1.08 / 1.01 (G1), and the lowest single cell is G1's 0.99 on Std with the reference engine.}
\li{\strong{Eight workers do not shorten these short runs on this host today}: with the machine on Init, Serial needs 6.5 s of wall clock with one worker and 9.1 s with eight (CPU 11.0 s against 18.8 s; the minimum of the 8-worker runs is 5.4 s). Probable causes, not separated here: the JVM launcher's warm-up (eight workers executing tier-3 code while one C2 thread compiles) and jobs outside the lock on the same CPUs. It is not a collector effect, but it is the background against which "wall clock with 8 workers" has to be read here.}
\li{The single Epsilon and "ZGC, no -Xms" runs on Init at eight workers are one run each (first batch) and show the 8-worker noise, nothing else (Epsilon 1.67 with the machine, 0.87 with the reference engine).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.2 Efficiency cores, no lock}

\p{All eight configurations, three repetitions on Init and Std; mathlib: one timed run per collector (Serial two or three for the machine) plus the logging run of section 9.}

