\tr{\td{mathlib, machine, 8 w}\td{3}\td{3 \%}\td{3 \%}\td{3 \%}\td{3 \%}}
}

\p{Reading (CPU time first):}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Resolution.} Two runs of the same configuration differ by 1 to 8 \% in CPU time with one worker and by 9 to 31 \% with eight (last table; on mathlib, where a run is long, by 0.3 \%); the one-worker instruction count repeats within 0 to 5 \%. A ratio between 0.95 and 1.05 is "the same" in every 8-worker cell of Init and Std, and so is any single ratio in the first table's 8-worker columns that the geometric means and the one-worker columns do not back up.}
\li{\strong{Parallel and G1 are indistinguishable from Serial}: CPU 1.02 / 1.02 of Serial with the machine at one worker, 1.03 / 1.03 with the reference engine; instructions 1.02 / 1.02 and 1.00 / 1.00. With eight workers the means are 0.95 / 1.02 (machine) and 0.98 / 0.93 (reference), inside the 8-worker noise.}
\li{\strong{ZGC costs a little}: 1.07 (machine) and 1.09 (reference) of Serial's CPU time at one worker, 1.07 and 1.00 at eight, with the heap committed at start; 1.01 / 1.05 and 1.05 / 1.01 without \code{\verb~|-Xms~}. Its wall clock on the short runs is 1.27 of Serial's with the reference engine at eight workers, because of that commit.}
\li{\strong{Shenandoah costs clearly more than the noise, in both modes and on both engines}: CPU 1.16 / 1.20 (machine, one worker), 1.19 / 1.21 (reference, one worker), 1.14 / 1.18 and 1.14 / 1.22 at eight workers; instructions about +20 \% (1.20 / 1.19 at one worker).}
\li{\strong{Epsilon, which never collects, is not faster than Serial} (1.01 and 1.05 at one worker): there is nothing to save (section 4).}
\li{\strong{Peak RSS} is lowest with Serial or G1 on the reference engine; Parallel, ZGC and both Shenandoah modes use 1.2 to 2.9 times Serial's on Init and Std (they let the heap fill further before the first collection; ZGC also has uncompressed pointers). With the machine engine the collectors are within 10 \% of Serial on Init, except Parallel at eight workers (1.6x); on Std, Parallel is 17 \% above Serial and ZGC 20 to 25 \% below.}
}
}
}

