\subtree{
\title{4. What a collector has to do on this workload}

\p{From the logging runs (efficiency cores; the allocation volume is the Serial log's sum of "used before - used after the previous collection"; the Epsilon column is the timed runs' peak RSS = everything ever allocated plus the VM):}

\table{
\tr{\th{input, engine, workers}\th{allocated GiB}\th{Serial: collections (full)}\th{pause total s}\th{share of the run's CPU time}\th{VM thread CPU s}\th{G1: collections}\th{pause total s}\th{Epsilon peak RSS MiB}}
\tr{\td{Init, machine, 1}\td{< 0.8 (no collection)}\td{0 (0)}\td{0.00}\td{0.00 \%}\td{0.00}\td{0}\td{0.00}\td{575}}
\tr{\td{Init, machine, 8}\td{< 0.8 (no collection)}\td{0 (0)}\td{0.00}\td{0.00 \%}\td{0.00}\td{0}\td{0.00}\td{746}}
\tr{\td{Init, reference, 1}\td{4.0}\td{5 (0)}\td{0.13}\td{0.42 \%}\td{0.10}\td{4}\td{0.04}\td{4696}}
\tr{\td{Init, reference, 8}\td{4.8}\td{6 (0)}\td{0.28}\td{0.69 \%}\td{0.18}\td{4}\td{0.23}\td{5138}}
\tr{\td{Std, machine, 1}\td{0.8}\td{1 (0)}\td{0.10}\td{0.30 \%}\td{0.08}\td{0}\td{0.00}\td{972}}
\tr{\td{Std, machine, 8}\td{0.8}\td{1 (0)}\td{0.13}\td{0.21 \%}\td{0.10}\td{0}\td{0.00}\td{1150}}
\tr{\td{Std, reference, 1}\td{7.1}\td{9 (0)}\td{0.17}\td{0.37 \%}\td{0.16}\td{7}\td{0.06}\td{7858}}
\tr{\td{Std, reference, 8}\td{8.0}\td{10 (0)}\td{0.35}\td{0.63 \%}\td{0.32}\td{7}\td{0.21}\td{8332}}
\tr{\td{Cedar slice, machine, 8}\td{0.8}\td{1 (0)}\td{0.07}\td{0.29 \%}\td{0.08}\td{0}\td{0.00}\td{906}}
\tr{\td{Cedar slice, reference, 8}\td{1.6}\td{2 (0)}\td{0.20}\td{0.88 \%}\td{0.13}\td{1}\td{0.05}\td{1966}}
\tr{\td{reachability, machine, 8}\td{0.8}\td{1 (0)}\td{0.13}\td{0.94 \%}\td{0.13}\td{0}\td{0.00}\td{1424}}
\tr{\td{reachability, reference, 8}\td{3.9}\td{5 (0)}\td{0.68}\td{3.01 \%}\td{0.87}\td{2}\td{0.01}\td{4023}}
\tr{\td{mathlib, machine, 8}\td{8.4}\td{9 (0)}\td{1.23}\td{0.37 \%}\td{0.71}\td{5}\td{0.17}\td{9244}}
\tr{\td{mathlib, reference, 8}\td{188.0}\td{236 (3)}\td{7.32}\td{0.69 \%}\td{6.39}\td{142}\td{0.53}\td{does not fit}}
}

\p{Reading:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The machine engine gives a collector almost nothing to do.} It keeps its working set in \code{\verb~|int[]~} tables that are pooled and reset; on Init it allocates less than the 1 GB young generation in the whole run, so \strong{no collector runs a single collection} (Serial, Parallel, G1, ZGC, Shenandoah alike: 0). On Std, the Cedar slice and reachability there is one young collection, on mathlib nine (8.4 GiB allocated in a run of 340 CPU seconds; 1.2 s of pauses on loaded efficiency cores).}
\li{\strong{The reference engine allocates 4 to 8 GiB on Init / Std and 188 GiB on mathlib}, almost all of it dead at the next young collection: 5 to 10 young collections on Init / Std, 233 young + 3 full on mathlib.}
\li{\strong{Serial's pauses are 0.2 \% to 0.9 \% of the run's CPU time in every cell but one}, mathlib included (7.3 s of 1,065 s). The exception is reachability with the reference engine (0.68 s in five young collections, 3 \%: a large live set is copied each time; G1 needs 0.01 s for the same run). That is the whole budget a better collector could win back in CPU time. It is smaller than the difference between two runs of the same configuration (section 2).}
\li{Epsilon (no collection at all) therefore cannot be faster by more than that, and it is not (section 2); it pays for fresh pages instead (kernel time: reference Init 8 workers, 2.4 s system time against 0.8 s) and its peak RSS is the allocation volume (4.7 to 8.3 GB on the reference engine). On mathlib it cannot run the reference engine (188 GiB).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5. Where the differences between collectors come from}

\p{With nothing to collect, a collector shows up through what it adds to the mutator and to the process, not through its collections.}

\p{\strong{5.1 Barriers in compiled code.} Serial and Parallel share one barrier (a card mark on reference stores); G1 adds its own pre- and post-write barriers; ZGC and Shenandoah add a barrier on every reference \strong{load} and ZGC also a store barrier (generational). The one-worker cells are the clean place to read them (no competition for cores between workers and the JIT compiler; the instruction count of a one-worker run repeats within a few percent). Efficiency cores, all four small inputs:}

\table{
\tr{\th{ratio to Serial, 1 worker, geometric mean over the cells}\th{machine: instructions}\th{machine: workers' CPU}\th{machine: process CPU}\th{reference: instructions}\th{reference: workers' CPU}\th{reference: process CPU}}
\tr{\td{Parallel}\td{1.02}\td{1.02}\td{1.02}\td{1.00}\td{1.02}\td{1.03}}
\tr{\td{G1}\td{1.02}\td{1.03}\td{1.02}\td{1.00}\td{1.04}\td{1.03}}
\tr{\td{ZGC}\td{1.02}\td{1.03}\td{1.07}\td{1.04}\td{1.07}\td{1.09}}
\tr{\td{Shenandoah}\td{1.20}\td{1.12}\td{1.16}\td{1.19}\td{1.23}\td{1.19}}
\tr{\td{Shenandoah gen.}\td{1.23}\td{1.16}\td{1.20}\td{1.19}\td{1.21}\td{1.21}}
\tr{\td{Epsilon (no barrier at all)}\td{1.02}\td{1.01}\td{1.01}\td{1.00}\td{1.08}\td{1.05}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{ZGC's barriers are close to free on this code}: instructions 1.02 (machine) and 1.04 (reference) of Serial's; CPU 1.07 and 1.09, of which the heap commit of 5.4 is the larger part ("ZGC, no -Xms": 1.01 and 1.05, on Init and Std). The machine's hot loops read and write \code{\verb~|int[]~} elements, which carry no barrier; the barrier is paid on loading the array references from the session object (that C2 keeps those loads out of the loops is an inference from these numbers, not something read off the generated code).}
\li{\strong{Shenandoah's are not}: instructions 1.20 / 1.19 and CPU time 1.16 / 1.19 of Serial's (machine / reference), about the same in generational mode, with no collection running at all on the machine engine on Init. The workers' own CPU time rises to 1.12 (machine) and 1.23 (reference); the rest is the compiler (5.2) and Shenandoah's control threads (5.3). That the int-array machine pays as much as the object-graph reference engine was not expected; it was not investigated further (a guess, not a measurement: the load-reference barrier is expanded late in C2 and keeps array-reference loads from being hoisted; \code{\verb~|-XX:+UseCondCardMark~} and the card barrier of the generational mode add a store-side cost).}
\li{\strong{G1 costs nothing measurable here} although its write barrier is the heaviest of the three stop-the-world collectors (consistent with reference stores being rare on both engines' hot paths; not checked in the code).}
\li{Epsilon has no barrier and no collection; it is the floor, and it sits within the noise of Serial.}
}

\p{\strong{5.2 The JIT compiler.} C2 has to expand and optimise the barriers, and with Shenandoah that costs visibly more compile time (CPU seconds of the two compiler threads in the logging runs, efficiency cores):}

\table{
\tr{\th{C1 + C2 CPU s}\th{Serial}\th{G1}\th{ZGC}\th{Shenandoah}\th{Shenandoah gen.}}
\tr{\td{Init, machine, 1 worker}\td{9.0}\td{8.9}\td{8.4}\td{10.5}\td{11.9}}
\tr{\td{Std, machine, 8 workers}\td{8.7}\td{9.1}\td{10.2}\td{14.0}\td{10.3}}
\tr{\td{Init, reference, 1 worker}\td{5.4}\td{5.6}\td{6.0}\td{6.3}\td{6.6}}
\tr{\td{Std, reference, 8 workers}\td{5.6}\td{5.5}\td{6.4}\td{7.4}\td{7.5}}
\tr{\td{mathlib, machine, 8 workers}\td{19.0}\td{19.6}\td{17.7}\td{21.0}\td{26.4}}
\tr{\td{mathlib, reference, 8 workers}\td{12.3}\td{11.2}\td{11.3}\td{12.0}\td{13.9}}
}

\p{The JIT is a large part of a short run whatever the collector (5 to 12 of 22 to 60 CPU seconds on Init and Std here), and with several workers a slower C2 has a second-order cost: the workers execute tier-3 code for longer.}

\p{\strong{5.3 The collectors' own threads} (CPU seconds in the logging runs; Serial collects in the VM thread):}

\table{
\tr{\th{GC threads CPU s (Serial: VM thread)}\th{Serial}\th{Parallel}\th{G1}\th{ZGC}\th{Shenandoah}\th{Shenandoah gen.}}
\tr{\td{Init, machine, 8 workers}\td{0.00}\td{0.00}\td{0.00}\td{0.05}\td{0.12}\td{0.47}}
\tr{\td{Std, machine, 8 workers}\td{0.10}\td{0.28}\td{0.00}\td{0.09}\td{0.21}\td{0.67}}
\tr{\td{Init, reference, 8 workers}\td{0.18}\td{0.47}\td{0.25}\td{0.13}\td{0.53}\td{0.56}}
\tr{\td{Std, reference, 8 workers}\td{0.32}\td{0.91}\td{0.21}\td{0.12}\td{0.28}\td{0.45}}
\tr{\td{Std, reference, 1 worker}\td{0.16}\td{0.69}\td{0.26}\td{0.32}\td{0.84}\td{1.95}}
\tr{\td{mathlib, machine, 8 workers}\td{0.71}\td{0.67}\td{0.28}\td{0.45}\td{1.71}\td{2.87}}
\tr{\td{mathlib, reference, 8 workers}\td{6.39}\td{17.39}\td{1.01}\td{2.84}\td{5.77}\td{7.53}}
}

\p{The concurrent collectors' threads are cheap in absolute terms because there is little live data to mark (the reference engine's live set on Init / Std is a few hundred MB): below 1 s on the small inputs for G1, ZGC and single-generation Shenandoah, up to 2 s for generational Shenandoah (its regulator and control threads run continuously). On mathlib with the reference engine the picture inverts for the stop-the-world collectors: Serial's VM thread needs 6.4 s and Parallel's four workers 17.4 s (9 full collections, the longest pause 1.3 s), while G1 does the same work in 1.0 s of collector CPU and ZGC in 2.8 s; Shenandoah needs 5.8 s. None of this is more than 1.6 \% of the run's 1,060 CPU seconds.}

\p{\strong{5.4 ZGC: the heap is committed at start, and pointers are not compressed.} With \code{\verb~|-Xms~} equal to \code{\verb~|-Xmx~} (the launcher's default heap flags are \code{\verb~|-Xms13g -Xmx13g~}) ZGC allocates and zeroes the whole heap before the first line of Java runs: 1.0 to 1.1 s of wall clock for 8 GB in the logging runs (3.0 s in one run while the host was swapping), 1.4 to 2.4 s of system time on the arena CPUs (1.76 s against 0.35 s for Serial on Init, machine, 8 workers), about 10 G kernel instructions that the arena's metric (\code{\verb~|instructions:u~}) does not see, and the memory is charged to the container from the first second although GNU time's peak RSS does not show it (RSS counts pages a thread touched). The row "ZGC, no -Xms" removes that cost. Independently, ZGC cannot use compressed object pointers: the reference engine, whose data are objects, has a peak RSS of 2494 MiB on Init (1 worker) against 1158 MiB with Serial and 5086 against 1695 MiB on Std (8 workers) (part of that is ZGC letting the heap fill to 10 / 20 / 30 \% before its warm-up collections); the machine engine, whose data are \code{\verb~|int[]~}, is unaffected (727 against 752 MiB).}

\p{\strong{5.5 Process exit.} Tearing down the address space is charged to whichever thread exits last (the sampler sees it under names such as \code{\verb~|Finalizer~} or \code{\verb~|Common-Cleaner~}): 0.05 s for a 0.75 GB process, 0.5 s for Epsilon's 5 GB, 1.0 to 1.1 s for ZGC's committed 8 GB. It is kernel time: in the CPU time, not in the instruction count.}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. Wall clock with 8 workers}

\p{Asked directly: does any collector change the wall clock with 8 workers?}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Not measurably on Init and Std, and not for the better.} On the arena CPUs the 8-worker wall ratios of Parallel and G1 are 1.0 to 1.1, those of ZGC and Shenandoah 1.2 to 1.3 (2.1); on the efficiency cores they scatter around 1 by as much as two runs of the same configuration differ (2.2). The bound from first principles is tighter than the measurement: Serial's pauses total 0.1 to 0.35 s per run (0.5 to 2 \% of a 15 to 20 s wall clock on the efficiency cores), and on Init with the machine engine there is no pause to remove.}
\li{\strong{On mathlib the bound is 1.2 s of 90 s (machine) and 7.3 s of 270 s (reference)}: about 1 \% and 3 \%. A parallel or concurrent collector can recover at most that, and the concurrent ones spend it again on barriers: with the machine on the arena CPUs the wall clock is 1.01 (Parallel), 1.02 (G1), 1.05 (ZGC), 1.15 and 1.17 (Shenandoah) of Serial's (section 7).}
\li{\strong{What a loaded host adds, and a quiet runner does not:} every stop-the-world pause first has to bring all worker threads to a safepoint, and a thread that is runnable but not on a CPU cannot get there. In the Serial logging run of the reference engine on mathlib the pauses sum to 7.3 s but the safepoints to 57 s; for G1 0.5 s and 25 s. This penalises collectors with many pauses on THIS host; it is not a property of the collector, and it is the main reason the wall-clock columns here cannot rank them.}
\li{ZGC with \code{\verb~|-Xms~} adds a constant 1 to 3 s to every run before the checker starts (5.4): on an Init run of 5 to 15 s that is the largest wall-clock effect in this report, and it is negative.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. mathlib}

\p{\code{\verb~|work/exports/mathlib.ndjson~}, 8 workers, heap \code{\verb~|-Xms12g -Xmx12g~}, efficiency cores. One timed run per collector (Serial: three for the machine) and one logging run; the full tables, logging runs included, are in section 9. A machine run on mathlib had been reported only for v0 on the native image (\code{\verb~|work/status/machine-M6.log~}, 13:30Z); with the switch set of this report it works on the JVM launcher too: every run accepted, 714,712 units by the machine, 0 declined. Epsilon cannot run the reference engine here (188 GiB allocated); it can run the machine (one run beside a Serial run: exit status 0, 355 CPU s = 1.00 of Serial's, peak RSS 9244 MiB against 4195): even with no collector at all the machine is not faster.}

\p{CPU seconds of the two runs of each configuration (timed run / logging run) and their ratio to Serial's:}

