\p{The machine once more on the \strong{arena CPUs under the lock} (one run per collector, each run its own batch of 95 to 100 s; the reference engine was not run there: one run would hold the lock for more than four minutes):}

\table{
\tr{\th{machine, mathlib, 8 workers, arena CPUs}\th{Serial}\th{Parallel}\th{G1}\th{ZGC}\th{Shenandoah}\th{Shenandoah gen.}}
\tr{\td{CPU s}\td{174.5}\td{176.1}\td{178.1}\td{180.8}\td{203.6}\td{207.3}}
\tr{\td{wall s}\td{94.5}\td{95.1}\td{96.7}\td{98.9}\td{109.1}\td{110.7}}
\tr{\td{peak RSS MiB}\td{4162}\td{4367}\td{4605}\td{5644}\td{7229}\td{7858}}
\tr{\td{instructions G}\td{1669}\td{1664}\td{1703}\td{1762}\td{2012}\td{1948}}
\tr{\td{workers' CPU s}\td{143.9}\td{145.3}\td{147.0}\td{147.2}\td{168.7}\td{171.9}}
}

\p{This is the cleanest series of the report (load average 33 to 43, one process family on the set, every run within two seconds of the same load and check phases): \strong{Parallel 1.01, G1 1.02, ZGC 1.04, Shenandoah 1.17, generational Shenandoah 1.19 of Serial's CPU time; wall clock 1.01, 1.02, 1.05, 1.15, 1.17; peak RSS 1.05, 1.11, 1.36, 1.74, 1.89.} The order and the sizes are those of the efficiency cores. (The wall clock itself, 95 s for 175 CPU seconds with eight workers, says that the workers had about two CPUs on average during the check phase, 79 to 96 s for 144 to 172 s of worker CPU; on the efficiency cores they had four. Whether that is other jobs on the arena cores or the launcher's own pipeline was not separated; it is the same for every collector.)}

\ul{
\li{\strong{CPU time repeats, wall clock does not.} The first two Serial runs of the machine differ by 0.3 \% in CPU time (345.4 and 346.5 s; a third one, two hours later with two CPUs of the set shared, needed 356.2 s); the two reference runs by 0.4 \% (1,060 and 1,065 s) while their wall clock was 273 s and 435 s. From 16:14Z another profiler's jobs were pinned to two of the eight CPUs of this set (10 and 11), and the load average moved between 57 and 151. The wall-clock rows are printed for completeness; they do not rank collectors.}
\li{\strong{Machine: nothing beats Serial.} Parallel and G1 are within 3 to 4 \% either way in both runs; ZGC costs 4 to 7 \% CPU (+7 \% instructions); Shenandoah 15 to 17 \% (+20 \% instructions), its generational mode 7 to 15 \%. Nine young collections in the whole run.}
\li{\strong{Reference: G1 is the only collector that was below Serial in both runs} (-3 \% and -6 \% CPU), with 0.5 s of pauses in total against Serial's 7.3 s and Parallel's 16.4 s (9 full collections, longest pause 1.3 s), 1.0 s of collector CPU against 6.4 s, and a peak RSS of 7.1 GB against 12.4 GB. The logs say why. Serial: after every young collection the 105 MB survivor space (\code{\verb~|-Xmn1g~}, SurvivorRatio 8) is full to the last kilobyte and the old generation has grown, by 143 MB on average: what eight workers hold live across a collection does not fit the survivor space, is promoted, dies later and stays until the 11 GB are full and a full collection runs (3 of them, 72 to 222 ms each: cheap in time, expensive in memory). G1: it sizes its survivor regions by need and frees dead humongous arrays (2 MB and more at its 4 MB region size) at every young pause (29 GiB of the 198 GiB allocated); its heap never exceeded 6.4 GB before a collection and it ran no old collection at all (140 normal young pauses, one concurrent start that was undone). Two runs are not enough to call 3 to 6 \% of CPU a result; the pause and memory figures are.}
\li{\strong{ZGC does not keep up with the reference engine at 12 GB on this host}: in the logging run 23 allocation stalls (workers blocked for 40.8 s in total, the longest 3.6 s) and 121 s of concurrent phases, because its two concurrent workers have to compete with eight busy workers (and here with other people's jobs) for CPUs. In the timed run it finished like G1 (222 s). No stall was seen with the machine engine.}
\li{\strong{Shenandoah} had one degenerated collection with the reference engine (allocation failure during a concurrent cycle), no full collection.}
}
}

