\subtree{
\title{10. Recommendation for the JVM launcher}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Keep \code{\verb~|-XX:+UseSerialGC~} as the launcher's collector.} No collector beat it in CPU time on any input, engine or worker count beyond the noise of the measurement, and it cannot be otherwise: its pauses are below 1 \% of the CPU time of every run but one (3 \%), mathlib included, and with the machine engine a run has zero to nine collections. It has no threads of its own, the cheapest barrier, the smallest footprint with the reference engine on the small inputs (with the machine, G1 and ZGC are 15 to 25 \% below it on Std), and it is the collector of the arena build, so a JVM-mode run exercises the same kind of collector as the native image.}
\li{\strong{Do not use ZGC or Shenandoah.} Both were measured on the real checker with parallel workers, as the owner asked. Shenandoah costs 15 to 26 \% CPU time (20 to 31 \% instructions) on both engines in both modes. ZGC costs 4 to 7 \%, takes 1 to 3 s to commit the heap at start under the launcher's \code{\verb~|-Xms = -Xmx~} flags, doubles the reference engine's footprint (no compressed pointers), and stalled the reference engine's workers on mathlib at 12 GB. Neither removes a pause that matters: there is none.}
\li{\strong{G1 is the one alternative worth keeping in mind, for one case}: the reference engine on mathlib-sized inputs in JVM mode, where it showed 0.5 s of pauses against 7.3 s, 1.0 s of collector CPU against 6.4 s, a peak RSS of 7.1 GB against 12.4 GB and 3 to 6 \% less CPU time in two runs. With \code{\verb~|-XX:ParallelGCThreads=4 -XX:ConcGCThreads=2 -Xmn1g~} it cost nothing measurable anywhere else. The cause is on Serial's side and may be curable there: with eight workers the 105 MB survivor spaces of \code{\verb~|-Xmn1g~} overflow at every young collection (section 7). A larger young generation or survivor space for multi-worker runs of the reference engine (\code{\verb~|-Xmn2g~}, \code{\verb~|-XX:SurvivorRatio=3~}) was NOT measured here; it is the first thing to try, on a quiet host, before considering G1, and the same question exists for the native image's serial collector, whose young generation is sized by its own policy. That is a note for the launcher's and the reference engine's owners, not a change proposed here.}
\li{\strong{Parallel: no.} Same CPU time as Serial, more memory, and on mathlib with the reference engine 9 full collections with pauses up to 1.3 s and 17 s of collector CPU.}
\li{\strong{If anyone does switch the launcher's collector, the heap flags must change with it}: \code{\verb~|-Xms13g~} is free for Serial, G1, Parallel and Shenandoah (pages are touched on demand) and costs ZGC a full commit; \code{\verb~|-Xmn1g~} was given to Serial, Parallel and G1 only (ZGC and Shenandoah size themselves; generational Shenandoah was not tried with it).}
\li{\strong{None of this applies to the arena build.} The arena runs the native image, and GraalVM CE's native image has exactly two collectors, serial and epsilon (G1 for native image exists only in Oracle GraalVM, which the owner has ruled out). Epsilon is not an option there either: the reference engine, which the driver needs as the fallback, allocates 4 to 8 GiB on Init / Std and 188 GiB on mathlib against a 16 GB runner; the machine alone would fit (9.2 GB on mathlib) and gains nothing from it (CPU 1.00 of the Serial run beside it, JVM mode). The collector question is therefore closed for the arena; what remains tunable there is the allocation volume itself and the serial collector's young-generation size.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{11. What was not measured, and caveats}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Most of the matrix is not from the arena CPU set.} The 4-worker cells, the Cedar slice, reachability, the Epsilon and "ZGC, no -Xms" rows, the logging runs and the mathlib repetitions are from eight efficiency cores without the lock (section 1). The Init / Std headline cells were repeated on the arena CPUs under the lock and agree (2.1 against 2.2); for the rest, ratios can differ on the X925 cores (four times the L2 cache).}
\li{\strong{The host was not quiet until 18:10Z} (load average 30 to 250), jobs that do not take the lock run on the arena CPUs too, and from 16:14Z two of the eight CPUs of the efficiency set (10, 11) were also used by another profiler's pinned jobs. CPU time of a one-worker run and of a long run repeats within a few percent; \strong{an 8-worker run of Init or Std does not} (spread table in section 2: tens of percent in CPU time and in the instruction count between identical runs). Why the instruction count of the same work moves that much with 8 workers was not investigated; candidates are the tier-3 code the workers execute until C2 has caught up (the compiler threads compete with eight workers for eight CPUs) and waiting in the driver. It matters beyond this report: the arena ranks by that count.}
\li{\strong{Repetitions.} Headline cells: 3 on the efficiency cores; 3 (one worker) and 5 (eight workers) on the arena CPUs. mathlib: 1 timed run per collector (Serial 3 for the machine) plus the logging run on the efficiency cores, and the machine once more on the arena CPUs. The other cells (4 workers; Cedar slice and reachability): one repetition (the instruction asked for three). Own ergonomics: one repetition.}
\li{\strong{Wall clock} cannot rank collectors on this host (section 6).}
\li{\strong{One batch of the lock's 49 held it for 132 s}, 12 s over the two-minute rule: a run estimated at 8.5 s took 29 s on arena CPUs that other jobs were using. The estimator had a bug (it never used observed run times); fixed at 16:31Z, and the limit lowered. All other holds were between 27 and 115 s (50 minutes in total; three hours were spent waiting).}
\li{\strong{Epsilon} cannot run the reference engine on mathlib (188 GiB allocated against 14 GB); with the machine it was run once.}
\li{\strong{Pause times and collector CPU} are from loaded efficiency cores; the thread sampler has a 0.1 s period.}
\li{\strong{Not measured:} the Graal compiler as host JIT (the launcher uses C2); other heap sizes than 8 / 12 GB (the launcher's default is 13 GB); other young sizes than 1 GB and the collectors' own; tuning flags of G1 / ZGC / Shenandoah; whether generational Shenandoah honours \code{\verb~|-Xmn~} (a row \code{\verb~|shengen-n~} is defined in \code{\verb~|gcbench.py~}, not run); the Cedar and cslib corpora in full; the native image (see section 10: it has no choice of collector).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{12. Files}

\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|work/prof/GC/scripts/gcbench.py~} (driver; \code{\verb~|--dry~} prints the batches), \code{\verb~|inner.py~} (in the container), \code{\verb~|run_e.sh~} (the steps on the efficiency cores; the arena phases were started by hand: \code{\verb~|gcbench.py A --cells ... --gcs serial,parallel,g1,zgc,shen,shengen --reps 3~} and \code{\verb~|--reps 5~} for the 8-worker cells, \code{\verb~|--limit 92~} to \code{\verb~|100~}), \code{\verb~|gclog.py~} (log parser), \code{\verb~|report.py~} (tables), \code{\verb~|build_doc.py~} (this file from \code{\verb~|work/prof/GC/GC.template.md~}), \code{\verb~|defaults.sh~}, \code{\verb~|diag_threads.py~}.}
\li{\code{\verb~|work/prof/GC/data/runs-e.jsonl~} (efficiency cores), \code{\verb~|runs.jsonl~} (arena CPUs, under the lock), \code{\verb~|runs-e-own.jsonl~} (own ergonomics), \code{\verb~|log-runs.jsonl~} (logging runs, with per-thread CPU), \code{\verb~|lock.tsv~} (waits and holds), \code{\verb~|defaults.txt~}; \code{\verb~|work/prof/GC/gclogs/*.gc.log~}; \code{\verb~|work/prof/GC/dist/~} (the distribution measured).}
\li{One row = one run: flags, exit status, \code{\verb~|cpu_time_s~}, \code{\verb~|wall_time_s~}, \code{\verb~|max_rss_bytes~}, \code{\verb~|instructions~}, \code{\verb~|instructions_kernel~}, \code{\verb~|user_time_s~}, \code{\verb~|sys_time_s~}, \code{\verb~|worker_cpu_s~}, verdict counts, \code{\verb~|load1_before~}, \code{\verb~|load1_after~}, batch and position in the batch.}
\li{Reproduce one cell: \code{\verb~|python3 work/prof/GC/scripts/gcbench.py X --cells machine:init:8 --gcs serial,parallel,g1,zgc,shen,shengen,epsilon --reps 3~} (under the lock, arena CPUs), or with \code{\verb~|--no-lock --cpuset 0-4,10-12~} as here.}
}
}
